From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 30 Jun 2026 23:01:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05155/11362] [outer-loop] correct stale 34x scipy claim +
 draft ARC value-head energy-distillation task

Operator asked whether ARC energy work is properly GPU-offloaded. Investigation: zero
torch/CUDA anywhere in the ARC value/search path (arc_value_learner.py,
arc_graph_explore.py, arc_competition_agent.py) -- correctly so, the per-node work is
small sequential numpy/scipy ops on tiny grids, the wrong shape for GPU (kernel-launch
overhead would dominate; the value head itself is a logistic regression).

Digging further surfaced an unverified live-Kaggle risk flagged in the code's own
docstring: a scipy.ndimage.label fast path vs a pure-python fallback 'the live Kaggle
kernel may lack it', claimed ~34x faster, never actually checked on the real sandbox.
Verified directly (carnot-arc-scipy-diag Kaggle kernel, real hardware):
  - scipy 1.16.3 IS present -- the fallback risk is CLOSED, not live.
  - The docstring's '~34x faster' claim was WRONG -- real measured difference is 1.41x
    (663us/call scipy path vs 936us/call fallback). Corrected the docstring in
    arc_value_learner.py to cite the real measurement instead of the stale claim.

ops/known-issues.md: drafted a precondition-gated, prototype-first task to attack the
CONFIRMED compute-cost bottleneck (exp4616: value-guided search wins at equal nodes,
loses at equal wall-clock) via energy distillation -- explicitly NOT re-proposing
representation (done, 0.725 AUROC), distribution-shift (exp4665 DAgger fix nulled
anyway), calibration (exp4616 ruled out), or call-frequency reduction (exp4652 lazy
top-k nulled). Anchored to the real 663us/call baseline instead of a hypothetical
number; requires an offline speedup+fidelity bar before any live A/B is attempted.

